104
00:05:19,940 --> 00:05:22,940
but we are in the midst of what is shaping up

105
00:05:22,940 --> 00:05:27,940
to be a possibly more widespread phenomenon

106
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:29,940
where councils are actually merging

107
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:31,940
into large area-wide councils-

108
00:05:31,940 --> 00:05:33,940
The Area 2 project. you may have heard about

109
00:05:33,940 --> 00:05:36,940
and probably read in Scouting Magazine this month-

110
00:05:36,940 --> 00:05:38,940
And we're right in the thick of it here.

111
00:05:38,940 --> 00:05:40,940
The Greater Michigan Council was just formed

112
00:05:40,940 --> 00:05:43,940
and we're going to be folded into that pretty soon.

113
00:05:43,940 --> 00:05:45,940
You're working with which troop there?

114
00:05:45,940 --> 00:05:48,940
I work with Troop 407, which is in Novi.

115
00:05:48,940 --> 00:05:51,940
We've been in business for close to 30 years.

116
00:05:51,940 --> 00:05:56,940
We have 58 scouts- actually 59 scouts currently-

117
00:05:56,940 --> 00:05:59,940
and we're expecting to add another 08 to 10,

118
00:05:59,940 --> 00:06:02,940
perhaps with the Weeblows crossovers this spring,

119
00:06:02,940 --> 00:06:05,940
back to our normal strength of about 68,

120
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:07,940
where we've been for the last- oh, I'd say

121
00:06:07,940 --> 00:06:09,940
three or four years,

122
00:06:09,940 --> 00:06:11,940
And we got there through a concerted effort

123
00:06:11,940 --> 00:06:14,940
of marketing to the Weeblows scouts.

124
00:06:14,940 --> 00:06:16,940
It does work. folks,

125
00:06:16,940 --> 00:06:20,940
You are currently serving as the committee chairman.

126
00:06:20,940 --> 00:06:22,940
That's right, I'm the committee chair.

127
00:06:22,940 --> 00:06:24,940
I've been in committee chair for five years.

128
00:06:24,940 --> 00:06:27,940
Prior to that, I was on the troop committee as a.

129
00:06:27,940 --> 00:06:30,940
my function was the Weeblows resource person

130
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:33,940
And I go back to Cub Scouts as Cub Master,

131
00:06:33,940 --> 00:06:38,940
Weeblows leader, Den leader and assistant Cub Master.

132
00:06:38,940 --> 00:06:40,940
What we hear from Frank on his blog

133
00:06:40,940 --> 00:06:43,940
is things concerning the troop committee.

134
00:06:43,940 --> 00:06:46,940
He's had some really excellent advice on there

135
00:06:46,940 --> 00:06:50,940
and a series of posts about running committee meetings

136
00:06:50,940 --> 00:06:54,940
and things like that that I think you will find very useful,

137
00:06:54,940 --> 00:06:56,940
And we'll make sure to link to his blog

138
00:06:56,940 --> 00:06:59,940
in the post that contains this podcast.

139
00:06:59,940 --> 00:07:01,940
We've got him here to talk about

140
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:05,940
the Scoutmaster troop committee relationship a little bit.

141
00:07:05,940 --> 00:07:07,940
Sometimes they're pulling against each other,

142
00:07:07,940 --> 00:07:09,940
but what we're always looking for

143
00:07:09,940 --> 00:07:12,940
is that kind of well-balanced relationship.

144
00:07:12,940 --> 00:07:14,940
We're getting the program to the boys.

145
00:07:14,940 --> 00:07:16,940
What's the best way, Frank,

146
00:07:16,940 --> 00:07:19,940
to get the committee chair and the Scoutmaster

147
00:07:19,940 --> 00:07:21,940
pulling in the same direction?

148
00:07:21,940 --> 00:07:23,940
Well, I think the easy answer to that

149
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:25,940
is that, since the committee chair is responsible

150
00:07:25,940 --> 00:07:27,940
for selecting the other adult leaders,

151
00:07:27,940 --> 00:07:30,940
he just needs to select a Scoutmaster he can work with.

152
00:07:30,940 --> 00:07:32,940
But that's not always possible.

153
00:07:32,940 --> 00:07:35,940
A lot of times the committee chair will come into a situation

154
00:07:35,940 --> 00:07:37,940
where a Scoutmaster's been around for a while

155
00:07:37,940 --> 00:07:39,940
and is entrenched.

156
00:07:39,940 --> 00:07:42,940
He doesn't have the opportunity to select,

157
00:07:42,940 --> 00:07:44,940
to hand pick a Scoutmaster

158
00:07:44,940 --> 00:07:46,940
and maybe doesn't have the experience to do so.

159
00:07:46,940 --> 00:07:50,940
So I think you really have to develop

160
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:53,940
a sense of cooperation between

161
00:07:53,940 --> 00:07:55,940
the committee chair and the Scoutmaster,

162
00:07:55,940 --> 00:07:58,940
And it's got to be mutual, so that each respects each other

163
00:07:58,940 --> 00:08:00,940
and hopefully each is on the same page.

164
00:08:00,940 --> 00:08:03,940
It really takes a clear understanding of each other's role

165
00:08:03,940 --> 00:08:05,940
and a willingness to really do your best

166
00:08:05,940 --> 00:08:07,940
in your own area of responsibility

167
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:08,940
in order to make things work.

168
00:08:08,940 --> 00:08:10,940
Because if there's friction between the two-

169
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:13,940
where the Scoutmaster wants to depart from the Scouting Program

170
00:08:13,940 --> 00:08:15,940
the way it should be done, for instance,

171
00:08:15,940 --> 00:08:19,940
or where the committee chair tries to push things around

172
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:21,940
that the Scoutmaster doesn't want to do,

173
00:08:21,940 --> 00:08:24,940
then you really won't have an effective troop for the boys.

174
00:08:24,940 --> 00:08:26,940
Let's start with you, Walter.

175
00:08:26,940 --> 00:08:28,940
What was your relationship with your committee chair

176
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:30,940
as a Scoutmaster?

177
00:08:30,940 --> 00:08:32,940
Well, my committee chair was somebody I worked with

178
00:08:32,940 --> 00:08:34,940
as a Cubmaster as well.

179
00:08:34,940 --> 00:08:37,940
We both moved on to the troop,

180
00:08:37,940 --> 00:08:41,940
And so we were already a good combination.

181
00:08:41,940 --> 00:08:43,940
Typically, I'm not very good at some of the things

182
00:08:43,940 --> 00:08:45,940
that committee chairs are supposed to do,

183
00:08:45,940 --> 00:08:48,940
so I'm glad to split the job up

184
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:51,940
and let them have their run.

185
00:08:51,940 --> 00:08:53,940
Right now we have an interesting situation

186
00:08:53,940 --> 00:08:55,940
where we have both the Scoutmaster

187
00:08:55,940 --> 00:08:57,940
and the committee chair stepping down,

188
00:08:57,940 --> 00:08:59,940
So we're looking for a new team.

189
00:08:59,940 --> 00:09:03,940
And, Tom, how about you?

190
00:09:03,940 --> 00:09:06,940
We've got a real good relationship

191
00:09:06,940 --> 00:09:08,940
with the committee chairman.

192
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:11,940
He understands what I'm trying to do,

193
00:09:11,940 --> 00:09:14,940
what the whole goal of the Scouts

194
00:09:14,940 --> 00:09:18,940
and the boy led,

195
00:09:18,940 --> 00:09:20,940
And he's behind it 100%

196
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:23,940
And it's very, very lucky

197
00:09:23,940 --> 00:09:25,940
that it's working out real well for me.

198
00:09:25,940 --> 00:09:27,940
And Larry, how about you?

199
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:29,940
I basically had three committee chairs

200
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:31,940
and two troops.

201
00:09:31,940 --> 00:09:33,940
Basically, we worked as a team.

202
00:09:33,940 --> 00:09:35,940
I had one for a couple years

203
00:09:35,940 --> 00:09:37,940
and we started a new troop

204
00:09:37,940 --> 00:09:39,940
And I had a guy that was there for eight years.

205
00:09:39,940 --> 00:09:43,940
He had four sons and he was really into everything,

206
00:09:43,940 --> 00:09:47,940
But he was very business-like

207
00:09:47,940 --> 00:09:49,940
and ran a very good meeting

208
00:09:49,940 --> 00:09:52,940
And before long he had about 20, 25 people in his meeting.

209
00:09:52,940 --> 00:09:55,940
The current troop that I just retired from

210
00:09:55,940 --> 00:09:57,940
had another great guy.

211
00:09:57,940 --> 00:09:59,940
Now in my committees I've always had

212
00:09:59,940 --> 00:10:01,940
probably slightly more committee members

213
00:10:01,940 --> 00:10:04,940
than assistants on campouts,

214
00:10:04,940 --> 00:10:06,940
So I've had a pretty good participation

215
00:10:06,940 --> 00:10:08,940
from committee members.

216
00:10:08,940 --> 00:10:11,940
We've all had some very good relationships

217
00:10:11,940 --> 00:10:14,940
with our committees, our committee chairs.

218
00:10:14,940 --> 00:10:17,940
How do we define what Scoutmasters

219
00:10:17,940 --> 00:10:19,940
and assistant Scoutmasters do

220
00:10:19,940 --> 00:10:21,940
and what the committee does?

221
00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:23,940
Let me throw that over to Frank for a minute.

222
00:10:23,940 --> 00:10:25,940
It's really fairly simple.

223
00:10:25,940 --> 00:10:27,940
Actually, we talked about this

224
00:10:27,940 --> 00:10:29,940
at a roundtable discussion last year

225
00:10:29,940 --> 00:10:31,940
which we called which lane are you in?

226
00:10:31,940 --> 00:10:33,940
We explored the subject and it's pretty simple

227
00:10:33,940 --> 00:10:35,940
to define what the committee's job is.

228
00:10:35,940 --> 00:10:38,940
If you look in, I think it's chapter four

229
00:10:38,940 --> 00:10:39,940
of the troop committee guidebook.

230
00:10:39,940 --> 00:10:40,940
it lists the things the committee

231
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:42,940
is directly responsible for:

232
00:10:42,940 --> 00:10:44,940
Things like ensuring qualified leadership

233
00:10:44,940 --> 00:10:47,940
is on hand and trained,

234
00:10:47,940 --> 00:10:49,940
Providing the meeting room and the meeting facilities

235
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:51,940
and making sure it's safe. responsible

236
00:10:51,940 --> 00:10:53,940
for finance, property,

237
00:10:53,940 --> 00:10:55,940
facilitating the outdoor program,

238
00:10:55,940 --> 00:10:57,940
supporting advancement, conduct boards of review,

239
00:10:57,940 --> 00:10:59,940
help with friends of scouting

240
00:10:59,940 --> 00:11:01,940
And the rest of the responsibilities.

241
00:11:01,940 --> 00:11:03,940
basically directly support

242
00:11:03,940 --> 00:11:05,940
the Scoutmaster in the program,

243
00:11:05,940 --> 00:11:07,940
Advised on policies of the BSA

244
00:11:07,940 --> 00:11:09,940
and the chartered organization,

245
00:11:09,940 --> 00:11:11,940
supports the Scoutmaster

246
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:13,940
in working with individual boys

247
00:11:13,940 --> 00:11:15,940
who might need a little bit of additional assistance

248
00:11:15,940 --> 00:11:17,940
and in general support the direct leaders.

249
00:11:17,940 --> 00:11:19,940
The Scoutmasters biggest job,

250
00:11:19,940 --> 00:11:22,940
also referred to in the troop committee guidebook,

251
00:11:22,940 --> 00:11:24,940
is to train and guide the boy leaders.

252
00:11:24,940 --> 00:11:26,940
Everything else pretty much falls under that heading.

253
00:11:26,940 --> 00:11:28,940
He works with the other leaders,

254
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:30,940
the other assistant Scoutmasters,

255
00:11:30,940 --> 00:11:32,940
to use the methods of scouting

256
00:11:32,940 --> 00:11:34,940
to achieve the aims of scouting.

257
00:11:34,940 --> 00:11:36,940
He meets with the parents

258
00:11:36,940 --> 00:11:38,940
to explain the program to them

259
00:11:38,940 --> 00:11:40,940
and he advises the patrol leaders council.

260
00:11:40,940 --> 00:11:42,940
So just as the interface between the adults

261
00:11:42,940 --> 00:11:44,940
and the boys is between the Scoutmaster

262
00:11:44,940 --> 00:11:46,940
and the senior patrol leader.

263
00:11:46,940 --> 00:11:48,940
the interface between the program

264
00:11:48,940 --> 00:11:50,940
and administrative half is between

265
00:11:50,940 --> 00:11:52,940
the Scoutmaster and the committee chair.

266
00:11:52,940 --> 00:11:54,940
That's where those two come together

267
00:11:54,940 --> 00:11:56,940
and our goal in scouting really-

268
00:11:56,940 --> 00:11:58,940
and you think about it- is not to manage

